\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard
What you are seeing is completely normal behavior for a dual-core microcontroller like the RP2350 when working with GDB and OpenOCD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here is why that message pops up even though your code is perfectly healthy:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 Why external reset detected occurs on cm1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Core 0 controls Core 1:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 When you type c (continue),
 GDB tells Core 0 (cm0) to start running your code.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Hardware Reset:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Part of the Pico 2's early boot sequence or your initialization code releases Core 1 (cm1) from its hardware reset state so it can sit in a low-power standby loop waiting for work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
OpenOCD's Perspective:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Because OpenOCD is independently monitoring the debug registers of both cores,
 it sees Core 1's hardware reset line toggle.
 It logs [rp2350.cm1] external reset detected simply to inform you that Core 1 just went through a hardware reset cycle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 The Breakdown of Your Log
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
[rp2350.cm0] halted...
 and [rp2350.cm1] halted...:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

This shows that OpenOCD successfully grabbed control of both Cortex-M33 cores at boot and parked them at the very beginning of the ROM bootloader (pc:
 0x00000088).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
[rp2350.cm1] external reset detected:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Core 0 booted up,
 subsystem clocks initialized and Core 1's reset line was toggled by the hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To verify that everything is 100% fine type Ctrl+C in GDB while it is running.
 If it cleanly halts both cores and lets you inspect your variables your debugging environment is flawless.
\end_layout

